    {
      "id": "f-sin-osc-2-d-rotation-11e9bd",
      "name": "FSinOsc2DRotation",
      "category": "Dsp",
      "signature": "FSinOsc2DRotation(In Starting Phase Radians)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "In Starting Phase Radians",
          "type": "Float",
          "default": "0.f"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "Void",
      "docs": "Generates a sine wave in the given buffer with the given frequency.\nUses the method of generating a 2D point on the unit circle and multiplying\nwith a 2d rotation matrix to advance the phase. Very accurate and very fast.\nUse this unless you're changing frequency every few samples on very tiny\nblocks (single digit sample count)\nDistortion vs directly calling FMath::Sin() is on the order of -100db, and is roughly\n10x faster (for constant frequency).\nFrequency changes are not interpolated across the buffer, so dramatic changes\ncould introduce aliasing due to a discontinuous derivative in the output.\nIf you're changing frequencies by a lot, constantly, you should probably\nuse VectorSinCos in a loop.\nUsage:\nconstexpr const int32 ChunkSampleCount = 480;\nalignas (sizeof(VectorRegister4Float)) float ChunkBuffer[ChunkSampleCount];\nFSinOscBufferGenerator Generator;\nGenerator.GenerateBuffer(48000, 400, ChunkBuffer, ChunkSampleCount);\nChunkBuffer now has 10ms of sine tone at 400hz. The next call will\ncontinue the sine tone where it left off."
    },
    {
      "id": "f-sin-osc-2-d-rotation-0cab61",
      "name": "FSinOsc2DRotation",
      "category": "Dsp",
      "signature": "FSinOsc2DRotation(Other)",
